1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A fixed-point number keeps the binary point in one place: n bits with f of them after the point leave i = n − f before it.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Each position carries a weight. With 8 bits and 3 fraction bits the weights run from 2⁴ = 16 down to 2⁻³ = 0.125.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
So the largest value is 2⁵ − 2⁻³ = 31.875 and the smallest step is 2⁻³ = 0.125. The step is the resolution.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The range is 0 to 2ⁱ − 2⁻ᶠ, not 0 to 2ⁱ, because the largest pattern is all ones and stops one step short.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Sign-magnitude spends the leftmost bit on the sign and keeps the other n − 1 bits as the magnitude: −5 in four bits is 1101.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,000
One's complement stores the negation as the bitwise complement. Two's complement complements and then adds one.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Two of the three have two bit patterns for zero: 0000 and 1000 in sign-magnitude, 0000 and 1111 in one's complement.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
So n bits give 2ⁿ distinct integers in two’s complement and only 2ⁿ − 1 in the other two. GATE 2016 Set 2 asks for the difference: 1.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
All sixteen four-bit patterns sit on a wheel in counting order. Two’s complement value is the unsigned value minus 16 once the top bit is set.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Negation is a mirror: flip every bit and add one. 0101 is 5, so 1011 is −5 — and doing it twice returns 0101.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
One pattern is its own negation: 1000 is −8, and negating it gives 1000 again. That is why the range is lopsided.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Widening a word copies the sign bit leftwards. −28 in sixteen bits is 1111 1111 1110 0100, not 0000 0000 1110 0100.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The adder does not know about signs. It adds bit patterns column by column and throws away the carry out of the top column.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Subtraction is addition of the negation: A − B is computed as A plus the complement of B plus one, so one adder and a row of inverters do both jobs.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Worked: 1111 1111 1111 0101 in sixteen bits. Invert to 0000 0000 0000 1010, add one to get 1011 = 11, so the value is −11.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The one-pass shortcut: copy the bits up to and including the rightmost 1, then invert everything to its left. Same answer, one sweep.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Two flags leave the top of the adder and they answer different questions. The carry out is a fifth bit; it matters only when the operands are unsigned.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Overflow is about the sign. It happens when two operands of the same sign produce a result of the other sign, and never when the signs differ.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The mechanical test: carry into the sign column exclusive-or carry out of it. Carry out alone is not overflow — adding two negatives always carries out.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
And overflow can happen with no carry at all: 0111 + 0001 = 1000 is +7 + 1 = −8. Read what the bits mean before choosing a flag.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
A floating-point number is scientific notation in base two: a sign, a significand with one digit before the point, and an exponent.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Normalising shifts until exactly one 1 sits before the point. That 1 is then always there, so IEEE 754 does not store it.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
The exponent is stored with a bias added: e + 127 in single precision, e + 1023 in double. So −3 is stored as 124, and 3 as 130.

24
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The bias also makes the bit pattern order agree with the numeric order, so two positive floats can be compared as integers.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Single precision is 32 bits: 1 sign, 8 exponent, 23 fraction. Double is 64 bits: 1, 11, 52. Biases 127 and 1023.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Decode S=1, E=10000001, F=1111 then zeros. E is 129, so the exponent is 2; the significand is 1.1111 = 1.9375; the value is −7.75.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Encode −14.25: binary 1110.01, normalised 1.11001 × 2³, exponent field 130, fraction 11001 then zeros, sign 1 — giving 0xC1640000.

28
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Both questions are the same four steps: sign, exponent field, significand, multiply. Learn the four steps, not the two answers.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
The exponent field splits the line into four regimes. E from 1 to 254 is a normal number; E = 0 is the denormal regime; E = 255 is infinity or NaN.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
With E = 0 the hidden bit becomes 0, so the significand is 0.F at a fixed exponent of −126. That fills the gap next to zero with evenly spaced numbers down to 2⁻¹⁴⁹.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,000
E = 255 with a zero fraction is infinity; with a non-zero fraction it is NaN. Zero has a sign bit, but +0 and −0 compare equal.

32
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The extremes of single precision: largest finite (2 − 2⁻²³) × 2¹²⁷ ≈ 3.4 × 10³⁸, smallest normal 2⁻¹²⁶, smallest denormal 2⁻¹⁴⁹.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Precision is not range. The significand holds 24 bits, so between 1 and 2 the gap is 2⁻²³ — and the gap doubles at every power of two.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Most decimal fractions are not exactly representable. One tenth in single precision is 13421773 / 2²⁷ = 0.10000000149.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Adding a small number to a large one aligns the exponents first, and the shifted-out bits are lost: 2²⁴ + 1 is exactly 2²⁴ in single precision.

36
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Rounding is to nearest with ties to even. So never test two floats for equality, and never assume the order of a sum does not matter.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
The procedure: name the format, name the width, find the sign, then decode or encode by weights. Four steps, in that order, every time.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
For a decode, write the four fields before computing anything: sign bit, exponent field, unbiased exponent, significand. Most marks are lost in the second.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Trap one: the bias, so E = 129 means exponent 2. Trap two: the hidden bit, so 1.F for normals but 0.F when E = 0.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Trap three: carry is not overflow. Trap four: a large hex constant is not automatically special — only E = 255 is.

